Introduction to Programming and Python
1. What is Programming?

Programming is the process of writing instructions that tell a computer what to do.

A set of instructions written to perform a specific task is called a Program.

Key Points
Converts requirements into computer instructions
Helps computers perform specific tasks
Uses programming languages to communicate with computers
2. What is a Programming Language?

A programming language is a language used to communicate with computers and create software applications.

Examples
Python
Java
C
C++
3. Programming Paradigms

A programming paradigm is a style or approach used to design and write programs.

Common Paradigms
Procedural Programming
Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)
4. Procedural Programming

Procedural programming focuses on functions, procedures, and step-by-step execution.

Characteristics
Function-based
Top-to-bottom execution
Easy to learn
Suitable for small applications
Less code reusability
Difficult to maintain large projects
Lower data security
Advantages
Simple to understand
Easy to write
Fast execution
Suitable for small programs
Disadvantages
Difficult for large projects
Less reusable code
Difficult maintenance
Lower data security
5. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)

OOP organizes programs using Classes and Objects.

It focuses on representing real-world entities using data and behavior.

Main Concepts
Class
Object
Encapsulation
Inheritance
Polymorphism
Abstraction
Characteristics
Class-based
Object-based
Code reusability
Data security
Easy maintenance
Easy scalability
Advantages
High code reusability
Better security
Easy maintenance
Easy scalability
Suitable for large applications
Disadvantages
More memory usage
Slightly slower execution
More complex for beginners